# Smooths: geom_smooth

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_smooth()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_smooth.html), which covers
`stat_smooth()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`geom_smooth()` draws a smoothed line through the data, with a band for its confidence interval,
to help the eye see a pattern among overlapping points. `stat_smooth()` takes the same arguments,
for drawing the fit with another geom. Left to choose, it fits a local regression, R's `loess()`,
when the largest group has fewer than 1,000 rows, and otherwise a generalised additive model,
`mgcv::gam()`; `method` names any other modelling function, such as `lm()`. Giggly has none of
this yet.

## A smooth through the points

```r
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth()
```

`mpg` has fewer than 1,000 rows, so the smooth is a `loess()` fit, with a band for its 95%
confidence interval.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()`, and the local regression it fits, R's `loess()`.

## Fitting along the y axis

```r
# If you need the fitting to be done along the y-axis set the orientation
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(orientation = "y")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()` and its `orientation` option.

## How wiggly the smooth is

```r
# Use span to control the "wiggliness" of the default loess smoother.
# The span is the fraction of points used to fit each local regression:
# small numbers make a wigglier curve, larger numbers make a smoother curve.
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(span = 0.3)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()` and its `span` option.

## Other models

```r
# Instead of a loess smooth, you can use any other modelling function:
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = lm, se = FALSE)

ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = lm, formula = y ~ splines::bs(x, 3), se = FALSE)
```

`se = FALSE` leaves out the confidence band. The second fits a linear model to a B-spline of `x`
from the `splines` package.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()` with its `method`, `formula` and `se` options, and the linear
models R's `lm()` fits.

## A smooth for each group

```r
# Smooths are automatically fit to each group (defined by categorical
# aesthetics or the group aesthetic) and for each facet.

ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, colour = class)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(se = FALSE, method = lm)
```

Mapping `colour` to `class` makes a group of each class, and a line is fitted to each.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()` with its `se` and `method` options and the linear models R's
`lm()` fits.

## A smooth in each panel

```r
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(span = 0.8) +
  facet_wrap(~drv)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()` and its `span` option. Giggly has the panels, with
`Giggly.Plot.facet_wrap/3`, where `facet_wrap(~drv)` is `facet_wrap(:drv)`.

## Logistic regression

`binomial_smooth()` is a plain R function returning a `geom_smooth()` layer that fits a logistic
regression with `glm()`. The `rpart` package's `kyphosis` records children who had spinal
surgery: their `Age`, in months, and whether a kyphosis was present afterwards, `Kyphosis`, a
factor of `absent` and `present`.

```r
binomial_smooth <- function(...) {
  geom_smooth(method = "glm", method.args = list(family = "binomial"), ...)
}
# To fit a logistic regression, you need to coerce the values to
# a numeric vector lying between 0 and 1.
ggplot(rpart::kyphosis, aes(Age, Kyphosis)) +
  geom_jitter(height = 0.05) +
  binomial_smooth()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_jitter()` and its `height` option, `geom_smooth()` with its `method` and
`method.args` options and the logistic regression R's `glm()` fits, and the `kyphosis` dataset
from the `rpart` package.

## Values between 0 and 1

```r
ggplot(rpart::kyphosis, aes(Age, as.numeric(Kyphosis) - 1)) +
  geom_jitter(height = 0.05) +
  binomial_smooth()

ggplot(rpart::kyphosis, aes(Age, as.numeric(Kyphosis) - 1)) +
  geom_jitter(height = 0.05) +
  binomial_smooth(formula = y ~ splines::ns(x, 2))

# But in this case, it's probably better to fit the model yourself
# so you can exercise more control and see whether or not it's a good model.
```

`as.numeric(Kyphosis) - 1` makes `absent` 0 and `present` 1. The second fits the logistic
regression to a natural spline of `x` from the `splines` package.

Not in Giggly yet: an expression in a mapping, such as `as.numeric(Kyphosis) - 1`, `geom_jitter()`
and its `height` option, `geom_smooth()` with its `method`, `method.args` and `formula` options
and the logistic regression R's `glm()` fits, and the `kyphosis` dataset from the `rpart` package.
